    meditation: `The intake form has fifteen fields. Name, address, date of birth, the nature of the matter, the relief sought, the opposing party, the prior counsel if any, the income for the means test. You can fill it out in nine minutes if the client is organized. The client is rarely organized. She comes in with a folder of papers in no order, a son who keeps interrupting her, and a story that begins, almost always, in the wrong place. By minute four you have decided what kind of case it is. By minute seven you have begun to plan the answer. By minute nine the intake form is complete, and you have missed what the client actually came in to tell you.

Proverbs is unsparing. "He that answereth before he heareth, sheweth himself to be a fool, and worthy of confusion." The Hebrew underneath the Douay carries a sharper edge — the word for fool here is the one for the man who has closed his ear, the man whose response is already loaded before the question has finished. Solomon, who will himself ask for an understanding heart in tomorrow's reading, sees the failure clearly. To answer before you have heard is not merely impolite. It is to advertise that you were never listening. The client knows. Even the disorganized client, the inarticulate client, the client who could not pass the bar exam in six lifetimes, knows when the lawyer in front of her has already moved on.

What the client is telling you is not always what the client is saying. The man who comes in for a divorce is, sometimes, not actually asking for a divorce; he is asking whether he is allowed to forgive his wife, and the legal vocabulary is the only one his hands know how to use. The woman who wants you to sue her employer is, sometimes, not actually asking for a lawsuit; she is asking whether what happened to her was real, because everyone she has told so far has implied it was not. The teenager whose mother brings him to your office for a juvenile matter is, sometimes, not the client; the mother is, and the case will not be solvable until you understand that. The intake form does not have a field for any of this. The form measures what the firm wants billed. It does not measure what the soul across the desk needs heard.

Newman, in a sermon to a London congregation that surely contained lawyers, names the duty in its most unglamorous form. "One of the most painful and trying duties of life is to listen patiently to others." He is honest. He does not pretend the duty is easy. He admits that the speakers are often tedious, that the subjects are often indifferent to the listener, that the material has often been heard a hundred times before. Then he asserts the duty anyway. "We owe it to all men." And he gives the theological reason that lifts the duty out of mere politeness. "We know not what God may be working in their hearts, of which their words, however unworthy they may seem, are the imperfect index." The client's words are an imperfect index. The lawyer's job is to read past the index to the page itself.

This requires a different posture than the one law school trained you for. Law school trained you to spot the issue. The client has barely finished her opening sentence and you have already identified the cause of action, the affirmative defenses, the statute of limitations question, the venue problem. You are good at this. The firm pays you for it. The trouble is that the issue-spotting reflex, useful as it is, runs on top of an assumption: that you already know what kind of conversation this is. The intake conversation is a conversation whose kind you do not yet know. The client has come in with one description of the problem and the actual problem is two questions deeper, and you will only get to the actual problem if you let the first description sit in the air for longer than the intake form wants you to.

Try, this week, a small discipline. When the client finishes her opening account, before you ask the legal follow-ups, ask one human follow-up. Not what you would expect — what happened next, what date, what document. Something slower. "What is the part of this that has been hardest to talk about?" Or, "What were you hoping I would understand by the time you finished telling me?" Or, when she trails off, simply: "Keep going." The discipline is to make the second minute of the conversation slower than the first. Almost every intake collapses at exactly this point, because the lawyer accelerates and the client follows. Decelerate. The client, if you decelerate, will tell you what she came to tell you. If you accelerate, she will tell you what she thinks you want to hear, which is almost never what is true.

The financial pressure on intake listening is real, and worth naming. The clinic has a backlog. The firm pays you to convert the call. The pro bono organization has a metric for matters opened per month. Every additional minute spent listening to a client whose case you may not be able to take is a minute the system would prefer you spent elsewhere. The system is wrong about this, in the long run, and you know it. The cases you have done worst on, over the course of your career, are not the cases you mishandled at trial. They are the cases you mis-intook. You did not understand, on the first day, what the client was actually asking for, and the case proceeded for two years on the wrong premise. The mistake at intake was small. The cost of it, four motions and a botched mediation later, was large.

There is also a deeper reason. The intake call is the moment the client is most vulnerable to you. She has come in carrying something — sometimes shame, sometimes grief, sometimes a confusion she cannot name — and she has, against her own instincts, sat down across a desk and begun to describe it to a stranger. If the stranger listens, she will, often for the first time, hear her own situation correctly. Lawyers underestimate how much of the early work of representation is performed simply by sitting still and letting the client speak. The client's case, in some real sense, has not yet been articulated. Your listening is what articulates it. You are not waiting to hear a finished problem. You are present at the moment the problem first finds words.

Newman's sentence ends with cheerfulness. "Bound to perform with cheerfulness." The cheerfulness is not pretense. It is the cheerfulness of a person who knows that the tedious speaker is, behind the tediousness, an image of God, and that God may be working in her heart something the lawyer is privileged to overhear. The intake call is, in this light, not the threshold of the case. It is the threshold of the encounter. Listen as if the case is the smaller of the two things you might miss.`,
    prayer: `Lord, you who hear our half-sentences and know what we meant before we found the words, slow my ear today. Let me not answer before I have heard. Make the next intake call a place where someone is heard for the first time in months, and let the lawyering follow from the listening, not the other way around.`,
    prompt: `In the last intake call you took, what did the client almost say but stop short of — and would you have caught it if you had given them another thirty seconds of silence?`,
